#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Talks to the security key for the lbssousa.security-key plugin.

The overlay runs this helper while it is open: one JSON command per line on
stdin, one JSON event per line on stdout. Everything it does goes through the
same tools a terminal would use (fido2-token, fido2-assert, pamu2fcfg, gpg,
ssh-keygen), as the user, without privileges. PINs live in memory for the one
command that needs them and are never logged.

A key is only registered where sudo and polkit will read it: the user's own
pam_u2f list, or the user's line of Omarchy's /etc/fido2/fido2, through this
package's helper (`pkexec omarchy-security-key-authfile`: the polkit agent asks
for the user to authenticate). Anywhere else, registering is refused, and
"target" in the state says why. Keys left in the user's own list while nothing
reads it are reported as "unused", so they can be removed.

Whether sudo and polkit ask for the key's PIN along with the touch is up to
their pam_u2f.so lines; "fido.pin" changes them through the package's other
helper (`pkexec omarchy-security-key-pam pin-on|pin-off`: polkit asks for an
administrator).

Commands:
  {"cmd":"refresh"}
  {"cmd":"fido.register","pin":"…"}
  {"cmd":"fido.remove","index":0}
  {"cmd":"fido.pin","require":true|false}
                                       (sudo and polkit ask for the PIN and a
                                          touch, or for a touch alone)
  {"cmd":"gpg.import"}                 (also updates a key gpg has: new expiry, subkeys)
  {"cmd":"gpg.trust"}
  {"cmd":"gpg.remove"}                 (the card's key out of the keyring: public
                                          key and the stubs pointing at the card)
  {"cmd":"ssh.import","pin":"…"}
  {"cmd":"ssh.create","pin":"…","options":{"algorithm":"ed25519-sk"|"ecdsa-sk",
     "application","user","verify","touch","comment","replace"}}
                                       (a new resident key, copied into ~/.ssh;
                                          "replace" answers ssh-keygen's question
                                          about a key with the same application
                                          and user ID, after "ssh-exists")
  {"cmd":"ssh.remove","name":"…"}      (one of sshKeys, with its .pub)
  {"cmd":"ssh.delete","name":"…","pin":"…"}
                                       (that key off the security key, for good,
                                          by the key handle of its copy; then the copy)
  {"cmd":"ssh.host","name":"…","enable":true|false,"minutes":0..1440 (optional)}
                                       (its line in ~/.ssh/config.d/<host>.conf)
  {"cmd":"ssh.persist","name":"…","minutes":0..1440}
                                       (connection reuse for that host: 0, the
                                          default, authenticates each connection)
  {"cmd":"cancel"}

Events:
  {"type":"state","devices":[{"path","name","rk","clientPin"}],
   "credentials":[{"handle","label","unused"}],
   "target":{"kind":"own"|"shared"|"no-pam"|"no-helper"|"other-file"|"mixed","path"},
   "pinRequired",
   "pinMode":""|"on"|"off"|"mixed",  ("" = neither uses pam_u2f; "mixed" = only one asks for the PIN)
   "pinHelper",                      (whether "fido.pin" can change it here)
   "sharedCount",
   "card":null|{"vendor","serial","holder","url","fingerprints"},
   "keyState":"none"|"missing"|"public"|"ready","trusted",
   "sshKeys":[{"name","application","host","hostState":""|"on"|"off"|"edited"|"foreign",
               "persistMinutes"}],
   "sshDefaults":{"user","comment"}}
  {"type":"busy","op","stage"}          (stage "touch" while waiting for a touch,
                                          "auth" while polkit asks for the password)
  {"type":"done","op",…}               (fido.register, fido.remove, fido.pin with
                                          "require", gpg.import,
                                          gpg.trust, gpg.remove, gpg.import with
                                          "updated", ssh.import with
                                          "imported"/"skipped", ssh.remove and
                                          ssh.delete with "name", ssh.create with
                                          "name"/"host"/"publicKey", ssh.host
                                          with "name"/"host"/"enabled", ssh.persist
                                          with "name"/"host"/"minutes")
  {"type":"error","op","code","detail"}
  {"type":"cancelled","op"}

It is also ssh-keygen's SSH_ASKPASS, run as `key-helper <prompt>` like any
askpass program: with SECURITY_KEY_ASKPASS_SOCKET in its environment (set only
for ssh-keygen) it asks the running helper for the PIN over that private
socket, prints it and exits.
"""

